/**
  @namespace Rodin::Adaptation
  @brief Mesh adaptation and deformation operations.

  @f[
    \mathbf{x}^{\prime}=\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{d}(\mathbf{x}).
  @f]

  @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Adapt "SWIFT::Adapt" and @ref
  Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Problem "SWIFT::Problem" fit mesh interfaces to level
  sets while controlling element distortion. @ref Rodin::Adaptation::DeformationMap
  "DeformationMap" and @ref Rodin::Adaptation::CellDeformation "CellDeformation"
  describe mapped geometry for evaluating deformed cells and fields. These
  components support moving-interface calculations and body-fitted mesh updates.

  # Design

  The module follows a strict separation of concerns:
  - The **level set** @f$ \phi @f$ owns the topology of the tracked domain
    (@f$ \phi < 0 @f$ inside, @f$ \phi > 0 @f$ outside).
  - **Classification** converts @f$ \phi @f$ into discrete cell attributes
    (see @ref Rodin::Geometry "Geometry" utilities such as the min s-t cut
    classifier).
  - **Geometry fitting** (SWIFT) moves and curves the mesh. It never
    decides topology.

  The SWIFT step solves a metric-preconditioned (natural-gradient) problem
  built entirely in the variational form language: a surface force
  (the Welsch first variation, assembled on interface facets) balanced
  against fitting and centered distribution metrics. Affine quadratic hinges
  recover quality in the direction problem. Hard admissibility
  (element validity) is enforced by a nonlinear line search.

  # Key classes
  - @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Parameters — budgets, tolerances and weights
  - @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Report — per-solve diagnostics
  - @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Problem — local fitting without mesh mutation
  - @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Adapt — owned P1 setup and in-place displacement

  @note Interface-fitting constraints are formulated as smooth penalties;
  the module deliberately avoids hard geometric projections inside the
  solve, which break line-search continuity.

  # Representative formula

  SWIFT combines fitting and distribution contributions in the displacement
  metric:
  @f[
    M=F+D.
  @f]
  The metric acts on displacement directions; admissibility is checked during
  line search. See @ref guides-swift "SWIFT" for the fitting model and contracts.

 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT
  @brief Robust interface fitting with a fitting--distribution metric.

  @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Problem computes displacement using
  @f$M=F+D@f$, quadratic affine quality hinges and Armijo globalization.
  @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Adapt owns a local P1 setup and applies valid
  results to affine simplicial meshes. MPI/PETSc execution is not implemented.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Heart
  @brief Cardiac models and their coupled state evolution.

  @code{.cpp}
  const auto report = model.step(dt);
  @endcode

  @ref Rodin::Heart::CCMLC2014T "CCMLC2014T" advances a reduced ventricular model
  through a cardiac cycle. @ref Rodin::Heart::HolzapfelReducedLaw
  "HolzapfelReducedLaw" and @ref Rodin::Heart::CCMLC2014PassiveLaw
  "CCMLC2014PassiveLaw" supply its reduced passive energy and stress response. The
  module supports cardiac-cycle studies and constitutive parameter calibration
  before larger three-dimensional simulations.

  0D models integrate ordinary differential equations over a heartbeat and
  are orders of magnitude cheaper than 3D simulations; parameters should
  round-trip between the 0D cycle and the corresponding 3D laws before
  debugging full simulations.

  # Representative use

  The reduced ventricular model advances a configured state with a nonlinear
  time step:

  @code{.cpp}
  using Model = Heart::CCMLC2014T<>;
  Model::Input input;
  Model model(input);
  const auto report = model.step(dt);
  @endcode

  Material, loading and initialization parameters in @c input must be chosen
  for the intended experiment before advancing the model.

 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Location
  @brief Spatial indexing and geometric search.

  @ref Rodin::Location::AABB "AABB" builds a bounding-volume hierarchy to locate
  physical coordinates in a mesh and recover the containing cell and reference
  coordinates. The resulting @ref Rodin::Geometry::Point "Point" can be used for
  field sampling and evaluation at arbitrary locations. The locator must be rebuilt
  when the mesh geometry or topology changes.

  # Representative use

  An AABB index first prunes candidate cells and then checks their inverse
  geometric maps. A query returns an optional mesh point:

  @code{.cpp}
  Location::AABB locator(mesh);
  const auto point = locator.locate(physicalCoordinates);
  @endcode

  The mesh must remain fixed during queries. Geometry or topology changes
  require rebuilding the locator.

 */

/**
  @defgroup RodinSerialization Serialization
  @brief Serialization and reconstruction of library objects.

  @code{.cpp}
  archive << values;
  @endcode

  Boost.Serialization adapters store arrays, Eigen-compatible dense matrices, flat
  containers, and optional values. The export registrations cover selected @ref
  Rodin::Geometry::ParametricTransformation "ParametricTransformation"
  instantiations used in MPI shard transfer. Including the relevant adapter headers
  enables binary archives of supported numerical data and container structures.

  # Representative use

  Include the adapter for a stored type before using it with a Boost archive.
  For example, a Rodin array can be written to a binary archive over a stream:

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Serialization/Array.h>
  #include <boost/archive/binary_oarchive.hpp>

  Array<Real> values(3);
  values << 1, 2, 3;
  boost::archive::binary_oarchive archive(stream);
  archive << values;
  @endcode

 */

/**
  @namespace Eigen
  @brief Eigen interoperability declarations for Rodin mathematical types.

  Rodin's matrix and vector classes provide Eigen traits and evaluators to
  participate in Eigen expressions, maps, and references.
 */

/**
  @namespace Eigen::internal
  @brief Eigen traits and evaluators for Rodin matrices and vectors.

  The traits, evaluator, and eval specializations connect Rodin-owned dense
  containers to Eigen's expression evaluation and storage interfaces.
 */

/**
  @namespace std
  @brief Standard-library customizations for Rodin types.

  Tuple traits enable structured bindings of Rodin linear systems and other
  supported library objects.
 */
